ng."]}, "overview": "Joe Wilson seeks another term in a South Carolina district that leans Republican by about seven points, and he cleared his primary without difficulty. His Democratic opponent, 25-year-old Zyon Khalifa, won a runoff in June and has emphasized local roots and military service, but his campaign has raised only about $19,000 and had under $1,000 on hand as of the latest filings. Wilson, by contrast, has raised roughly $861,000.\n\nEvery major race rater places the contest at Likely R or safer, and prediction markets give Democrats only a small chance of an upset. With no public general-election polling available, the forecast rests on district fundamentals, the money gap, and the expert consensus, all of which point to a comfortable Wilson win, though the absence of polling keeps uncertainty somewhat elevated.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0664, "margin": -9.56}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0576, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0526, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0819, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0438, "margin": -11.0}]}